-- Passage rg5-text-structure-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e156c62e-6b3a-58c1-906c-1c868a9a21da', 'rg5-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Keeping Cool in the Desert$q$, $q$Text A: Two Desert Animals

The camel and the fennec fox both live in hot, dry deserts. Both animals can go a long time without drinking water. Yet they stay cool in very different ways. A camel is large and active during the day. Its thick fur actually blocks the sun's heat from reaching its skin. The fennec fox, by contrast, is tiny and mostly comes out at night. During the hot day, it rests in a cool burrow under the sand. Its huge ears are another difference. They let extra body heat escape into the air.

Text B: What Heat Does to a Body

Desert heat can be dangerous for animals. When the air gets very hot, an animal's body temperature starts to rise. Because of this, the animal may lose water by sweating or panting. Losing too much water causes the body to become weak and tired. As a result, many desert animals have special ways to save water and stay cool. Heat is the cause, and these clever body features are the effect.$q$, 'draft')
