-- Passage rg5-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ff846cc6-a61a-5ab1-808c-c317b482ffe9', 'rg5-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Shaping Clay$q$, $q$Text A: Making a Clay Bowl

Making a clay bowl takes several days. First, the potter presses and folds the clay to remove air bubbles. Next, the potter shapes the bowl, either by hand or on a spinning wheel. Then the bowl must dry slowly for about a week. After it is dry, the bowl is baked in a very hot oven called a kiln. This first baking makes it hard. Finally, the potter paints on a glaze and bakes the bowl again to give it a shiny coat.

Text B: Two Ways to Shape Clay

Potters can shape clay by hand or on a wheel. Both methods start with the same soft clay, and both can make beautiful bowls. However, they feel very different to use. Hand building is slow and quiet. The potter pinches the clay or stacks coils of it like rope. Because it needs no machine, it is easy to try at home. Wheel throwing, on the other hand, is fast. The spinning wheel helps make bowls that are round and even. Yet it takes a lot of practice to learn.$q$, 'draft')
